  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first: without noise, learning rates above 2/3 make the x swings grow until the run diverges.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with spread 2·√((64−b)/(64·b)) for batch b, so it is exactly zero at 64.",
    "The orange arrow is the actual next step (learning rate × noisy slope, reversed); the grey arrow is learning rate × true slope, reversed. Both are drawn at true length (scale 1).",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6 or the ball leaves the drawn square (diverged). The ball is then held at the bowl's edge; the Loss readout keeps its true value.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed, run number and step. Reset replays run 1. New run keeps the last path as a ghost and uses the next noise values.",
    "Changing a setting mid-run applies from the next step on. With noise, a single step lowers the loss only on average."
  ]
}